  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia has supported Cambodia&#x2019;s health system for over two decades. Through the Health Equity and Quality Improvement Project Phase 2 (HEQIP 2) Australia aims to ensure all Cambodians can access quality health services without financial hardship. HEQIP 2 strengthens the health system and supports policy reforms aligned with Cambodia&#x2019;s new Universal Health Coverage (UHC) roadmap.
&#x200d;
Throughout 2024-25, Australia supported the Ministry of Health to launch national screening and treatment services for diabetes and hypertension in over 1,300 public health facilities. 
&#x200d;
HEQIP 2 contributed to treatment reimbursements, health worker training and community education materials. Australia also facilitated policy dialogues and analysis to advance UHC, including cervical cancer screening and digital health expansion.
&#x200d;
 HEQIP 2 also contributed to improved quality of care by introducing a more user-friendly assessment tool for primary health care, informed by independent review findings and supported by new coaching guides. As a result, key health indicators improved: women and children receiving nutrition services nearly doubled, skilled birth attendance rose by 44 per cent and early childhood immunisations increased by 44 per cent.
&#x200d;
Additionally, the new National Payment Certification Agency (NPCA) was institutionalised to independently certify health insurance claims and facility performance before reimbursement, reinforcing accountability and quality.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting Cambodian citizens in enhancing their understanding of government services and assisting local governments in making decisions informed by citizen feedback. Our support is delivered in partnership with local civil society organisations and the Cambodian Government through the Implementation of the Social Accountability Framework (ISAF). 
&#x200d;
ISAF supports local service providers to improve the quality and inclusivity of services, including education, health and local administration, by monitoring delivery against national standards. By December 2024, 88 per cent of ISAF-supported service providers were meeting national quality standards (compared to 46 per cent at the program&#x2019;s commencement).
&#x200d;
Local authorities are increasingly delivering services aligned with citizen priorities. Citizen satisfaction with public services consequently increased from 72 per cent in 2023 to 81 per cent by December 2024.  Furthermore, 93 per cent of target communes have allocated budget to community-generated activities as of December 2024, reflecting the growing value local government places on citizen engagement.
&#x200d;
ISAF&#x2019;s efforts also contribute to national policy reforms. In April 2025, the Ministry of Interior and the Ministry of Economy and Finance reformed sub-national government budget policy to allow more spending on social and environmental activities, enabling more responsive budgeting that reflects local priorities and citizen feedback.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting greater access to affordable healthcare through the expansion of social health protection measures under HEQIP 2. This project continues to implement the Health Equity Fund (HEF), which was successfully rolled out nationally during the first phase. 
&#x200d;
As of December 2024, 4.82 million Cambodians (approximately 27 per cent of the population) received free or subsidised care through the HEF scheme, covering both medical and non-medical costs, up from 2.9 million in 2023.
&#x200d;
This period also saw improved access to health services for the most vulnerable populations, with HEF utilisation increasing in areas with the highest poverty levels &#x2013; from 43 per cent in 2022 to 70 per cent of patients in 2024.
&#x200d;
HEQIP 2 also delivered policy analysis and recommendations to expand social health protection measures through a review of the HEF benefit package, in line with priorities outlined in the UHC roadmap.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health Equity Quality Improvement Project (HEQIP) II builds on H-EQIP 1 and supports the Royal Government of Cambodia (RGC) to achieve its 2030 goal of Universal Health Coverage (UHC). The aim is for all people to have access to the quality health services they need, when and where they need them, without financial hardship. During the initial phase, the project supported policy dialogues, analysis and review which is shaping the development of the RGC&#x2019;s roadmap for UHC and digital health priorities and informing the expansion of the Health Equity Fund benefit package.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Social Accountability Framework Phase 2 improved public service delivery in 465 local administrations, 372 health centres, and 2036 primary schools. In 2022, 72% of service providers supported by ISAF met key national standards (compared with 46% when the program just started), and 90% of citizens agreed that the Joint Accountability Action Plans, developed by citizens and service providers, satisfactorily captured their priorities to improve local services. Furthermore, 31 communes have started to allocate budget to ISAF. This is expected to increase to 600 in 2023. A new sub-decree is being developed to allocate budget for social services including ISAF.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
